From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: fix flow entry word count

0411 set PPE_FLOW_TBL_ENTRY_WORDS to 9, copied from
PPE_IN_FLOW_TBL_OP_DATA_ENTRIES (the width of the DATA register block
the flow table's indirect-op trigger writes through). That's wrong: 9
is the *capacity* of that register block, sized for the largest entry
type that shares it (an IPv6 5-tuple entry, union in_flow_ipv6_5tuple_
tbl_u's val[9] in the vendor SDK). A plain IPv4 5-tuple entry is only 5
words (union in_flow_tbl_u's val[5], non-APPE) -- confirmed by reading
both union definitions in the vendor's include/hsl/hppe/hppe_flow_reg.h
side by side, not just the one this driver actually needs.

Caught while starting the IPv4 flow-entry field-encoding work (the next
piece after 0411-0413's table-access primitives), before it built
PPE_FLOW_TBL_ENTRY_WORDS=9 into a real entry struct -- ppe_flow_entry_
add() itself has no bug (it just writes N words generically), so this
only needed a one-line fix, but a caller using the wrong entry size
would silently write 4 words of uninitialized stack past its own entry
into hardware. No caller exists yet for this to have affected.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_flow.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_flow.h
@@ -10,8 +10,15 @@
 
 #include "ppe.h"
 
-/* Number of 32-bit words that make up one IPv4 5-tuple flow entry. */
-#define PPE_FLOW_TBL_ENTRY_WORDS		9
+/* Number of 32-bit words that make up one IPv4 5-tuple flow entry.
+ * This is the entry's own size (union in_flow_tbl_u's val[5] in the
+ * vendor SDK for our non-APPE chip), not the wider 9-word capacity of
+ * PPE_IN_FLOW_TBL_OP_DATA -- that register block is sized for the
+ * largest entry type sharing this trigger mechanism (an IPv6 5-tuple
+ * entry needs all 9 words for its wider addresses); a plain IPv4
+ * 5-tuple entry only occupies the first 5.
+ */
+#define PPE_FLOW_TBL_ENTRY_WORDS		5
 
 /**
  * enum ppe_flow_op_mode - PPE flow table addressing mode.
